import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths

## The vibrate package: the phone's vibration motor from a handler (`Vibrate.once!`,
## `Vibrate.pattern!`), the plugin whose script is the browser's half (`Vibrator.plugin`), and the
## Android host's own half. The pomodoro example buzzes with it when a session ends.
meta = {
	title: "Vibrate",
	description: "packages/vibrate, a plugin for Roc Solid apps: the phone's vibration motor buzzed from a handler, once or in a pattern, in a browser (navigator.vibrate) and in an Android app on the native host.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "install", label: "Installing it" },
		{ id: "buzz", label: "Buzzing" },
		{ id: "browser", label: "In a browser" },
		{ id: "phone", label: "On a phone" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The ecosystem · vibrate"
				title="A buzz from a handler"
			>
				<code>{"Vibrate.pattern!([300, 150, 300])"}</code> buzzes the phone twice: 300 ms on, 150 ms off,
				300 ms on. The same line works in Chrome on Android and in an app on the
				<a href={Paths.examples_pomodoro}>Android host</a>.
			</PageHero>
			<p>
				Use this package when a phone should buzz in response to something: a timer that ran out, a message
				that arrived, a tap that needs to be felt. You call <code>Vibrate.once!</code> or
				<code>Vibrate.pattern!</code> from a handler, and the phone's motor runs. The
				<a href={Paths.examples_pomodoro}>pomodoro example</a> buzzes this way when a session ends.
			</p>
			<p>
				The package has two parts. <code>Vibrate</code> is a module that both halves of an app compile, so a
				component that buzzes type-checks on the server and in the browser. <code>Vibrator</code> is a
				<a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#plugins"}>plugin</a>: it serves a small script that every page
				loads, and that script is what passes the buzz to the browser. An Android app on the native host
				doesn't need the script, because the host runs the buzz itself.
			</p>

			<Heading id="install">Installing it</Heading>
			<p>Name the package in the app's header, and add the plugin to the app's plugins:</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.vibrate_header.file}, its header"} source={Sources.vibrate_header.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<Code title={"${Sources.vibrate_install.file}, in part"} source={Sources.vibrate_install.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>Then import <code>vibrate.Vibrate</code> in any page, component or module that buzzes.</p>

			<Heading id="buzz">Buzzing</Heading>
			<p>The whole module is two functions:</p>
			<Code title={Sources.vibrate_module.file} source={Sources.vibrate_module.rocx} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<code>{"Vibrate.once!(400)"}</code> buzzes once, for 400 milliseconds.
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>{"Vibrate.pattern!([200, 100, 200])"}</code> takes a list of durations in milliseconds,
					alternating on and off, in the form <code>navigator.vibrate</code> takes. This one buzzes, rests and
					buzzes again.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				A step longer than 10 seconds is cut to 10 seconds, and only the first 32 steps play.
			</p>
			<p>
				A buzz is a <a href={"${Paths.components}#handlers"}><code>Browser.call!</code></a> underneath, so it
				follows the same rules. It runs after the handler finishes and the page shows what the handler
				changed. Only a handler can buzz: an event's handler, an
				<a href={"${Paths.components}#effects"}>effect's</a> <code>apply</code>, or a timer's callback. Calling
				it while the page computes (in a hole, a memo or a component's body), or on the server, fails. The
				pomodoro's timer buzzes from its interval's tick, beside its chime:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.vibrate_use.file}, in part"} source={Sources.vibrate_use.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />

			<Heading id="browser">In a browser</Heading>
			<p>
				The plugin serves its script at <code>/rocx/plugins/vibrate/vibrate.js</code>. Every page loads it
				before <code>start.js</code>, so it is ready before the page hydrates. The script registers a browser
				effect named <code>vibrate.pattern</code> on <code>window.rocxEffects</code>, and that effect passes the
				pattern to <code>navigator.vibrate</code>:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.vibrate_script.file} source={Sources.vibrate_script.rocx} />
			<p>
				Chrome on Android buzzes. Desktop browsers, Safari and Firefox don't let a page use a motor, so on
				them a buzz does nothing, and nothing fails. A browser may also stay still until the reader has
				touched the page; that isn't an error either.
			</p>

			<Heading id="phone">On a phone</Heading>
			<p>
				On the native Android host, a plugin's <code>Browser.call!</code> goes to a native handler with the
				same name, and no script is involved. The host has two such handlers: <code>audio.play</code>
				(for <a href={Paths.ecosystem_audio}>audio</a>, a MediaPlayer that fetches the sound from the app's
				server) and <code>vibrate.pattern</code>, which plays the pattern on Android's <code>Vibrator</code>
				as a waveform. The app's manifest asks for <code>android.permission.VIBRATE</code>, which Android grants
				at install, so the user sees no prompt.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
